Seraphim wrote:

[challenging use of "all sorts of" to mean less than "all of"]

> Did someone change the meaning of "all" when I
> wasn't paying attention?

Apparently no one ever explained to you what
"colloquial usage" means.  Most often, it means the
words cannot safely be taken at face value or in
accordance with the dictionary definition, but
instead have a special meaning in the particular
phrase where they occur.

English, the world's largest language in number of
words, and probably the fastest growing one in
number of words added per time period, is much, much
too rich and mutable a language to trust dictionary
definitions to be more than guidelines, or to be all
encompassing.

So, yes, since "all" in "all sorts of" doesn't mean
"each and every one" but is instead part of a
colloquial usage where it means closer to "in many
instances", ye ol' rug o' definition has indeed been
pulled out from under your feet.

Notice also that even literally "all sorts of" means
"at least one of every sort", not "every instance of
each sort", but the colloquial meaning isn't even
that strong, it is understood that the phrase should
be treated as hyperbole, some "sorts" may be omitted
without "all sorts of" being incorrect colloquial usage.

